{"kind":"artifact","text":"Implementation of request 2a9f7da5 at cmd/gitseq-mcp@1843fd90bee09c37619df1996d7078635683f82a on branch request/invalid-advertisement-fail-closed, recut onto current main 4fc94b0f. Fulfils my promise e636faa5, alongside internal/app artifact 29ae11fb and cmd/gs artifact 77cd5d39.\n\nBEHAVIOUR IS UNCHANGED HERE. That is the point of this artifact, and the reason it exists separately rather than being folded into a sentence elsewhere.\n\nThe adapter shares the advertisement primitive with gs. When Workspace.ResidentURL was replaced by the tri-state ResidentAdvertisement, the adapter had to say something about the third state, and there were two ways to arrive at an answer. It could inherit one by accident — whatever falls out of the new return type — or it could make a choice and write it down. Codex's audit was explicit that it must be the second, and I agree: the failure this whole request repairs was a policy nobody chose, produced by a boolean that collapsed four distinct failures into one answer. Replacing one accidental policy with another would repair the symptom and keep the disease.\n\nSo room.endpoint() now reads the tri-state and explicitly treats anything other than AdvertisementPublished as no resident, with a comment giving the reason and pointing at docs/reference/gs/serve.md. The reason is that the adapter holds a live session it cannot refuse as a whole: gs is one command that can fail and tell an operator what to do next, whereas the adapter is a long-lived process whose caller has no such recovery path in the moment. That asymmetry is a real difference between the two surfaces, not an oversight, and serve.md now says the two disagree knowingly rather than leaving a reader to assume they agree.\n\nWHAT DID CHANGE HERE, and it is a strict improvement rather than a behaviour change: the adapter inherits the 8 KiB bound on the resident.json read. An oversized record was already going to be treated as absent or dialled; now it is bounded before being read whole.\n\nTESTS. No cmd/gitseq-mcp test changed, which is the evidence for the unchanged-behaviour claim rather than an absence of care: the existing suite pins the adapter's behaviour, and it passes untouched. ./cmd/gitseq-mcp is green at -count=1.\n\nCONDITION MET. Codex's audit: \"keep MCP policy unchanged only through an explicit adapter choice, not accidental bool collapse.\" Met by an explicit branch and a written reason at the adapter, not by the shape of the return type.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt clean; go build ./... and go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... over the whole module with zero failures; ./internal/docset green at -count=1.\n\nA REVIEWER SHOULD PUSH ON THIS ONE. I think the divergence is correct, but it is a judgment rather than a derivation, and it means two surfaces in the same repository now answer the same untrusted file differently. If you think the adapter should refuse too — start-time refusal, or per-call refusal with a named recovery in the tool result — that is a defensible position and I would rather hear it now than after this merges and the asymmetry hardens into precedent.","body":{"commit":"1843fd90bee09c37619df1996d7078635683f82a","path":"cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}